---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import FamilyMap from '../../components/FamilyMap.astro';
import Multiples from '../../components/Multiples.astro';
import Identity from '../../components/Identity.astro';
import Mixture from '../../components/Mixture.astro';
import Tex from '../../components/Tex.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
// The paper this page is built on lives in the publications collection, so its links stay in one place.
const paper = (await getCollection('publications')).find((p) => p.data.id === 'ca-taxonomy')?.data;
const doi = `https://doi.org/${paper?.doi ?? '10.1016/j.cnsns.2024.108362'}`;
const arxiv = paper?.url ?? 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.08408';
// One tab per family, between the recipe they all start from and the verdict; the hash picks the tab, so a family can be linked.
const tabs = [
  { id: 'recipe', name: 'The recipe' },
  { id: 'asynchronous', name: 'Asynchronous' },
  { id: 'stochastic', name: 'Stochastic' },
  { id: 'states', name: 'More states' },
  { id: 'neighbourhoods', name: 'Wider neighbourhoods' },
  { id: 'rules', name: 'More rules' },
  { id: 'verdict', name: 'Verdict' },
];
const links = {
  eca: '#definition', aca: '#asynchronous', sca: '#stochastic', msca: '#states', enca: '#neighbourhoods', nuca: '#rules',
  'aca-sca': '#id-aca-sca', 'sca-nuca': '#alloc', 'nuca-msca': '#id-nuca-msca', 'msca-enca': '#id-pool', 'enca-aca': '#id-sweep',
};
---
<Base title="Five ways to bend a cellular automaton" description="The five families of cellular automata beyond the elementary one, asynchronous, stochastic, multi-state, extended-neighbourhood and non-uniform, and the exact identities between them, live in the browser: my review paper in Communications in Nonlinear Science and Numerical Simulation." wide>
  <div class="measure">
    <h1>Five ways to bend a cellular automaton</h1>
    <p>
      The simplest cellular automaton is a row of cells, each 0 or 1. At every tick each cell looks at itself and its two
      neighbours, and one fixed rule, the same for every cell, says what it becomes next; all cells switch at the same
      moment. That is the elementary cellular automaton, and it has been studied to death, because even it can compute
      anything a computer can. Yet almost nobody who uses cellular automata uses this one. A model of a forest fire, an
      epidemic, a traffic jam or a cipher bends at least one item of the recipe: cells that do not all switch at once,
      rules that roll dice, more than two states, neighbours further away, different rules for different cells. Each
      bend grew its own name and its own literature, and from the outside the result looks like a pile of trial and error.
    </p>
    <p>
      In a <a href={doi}>review paper</a> with Kallil Zielinski, Aisling Daly, Odemir Bruno and Jan Baetens I sorted the
      pile. Its answer, in one sentence:
    </p>
    <p class="claim">
      The variants of the cellular automaton are not a melting pot but a family tree: five families, each bending one
      item of the classical definition, tied together by exact mathematical identities, and in which every gain in
      realism is paid for in mathematical elegance.
    </p>
    <p>
      Unlike in a biological family tree, some types belong to two families at once, because they are provably the same
      thing written two ways. The tabs below go through the families one by one, the way the paper does: the definition,
      the main variants within the family, the identity that ties it to its neighbour, and what the bend does to the
      tools that describe a cellular automaton. Every identity runs live, and the page counts, cell by cell, whether the
      two sides really draw the same picture.
    </p>
    <p>
      The paper is a review, so it has no data: everything below is simulated in your browser. The formulas and the
      examples are the paper's; where I add something of my own, I say so.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="tabbed">
    <nav class="tabs" aria-label="Families">
      <ul role="tablist" aria-orientation="vertical">
        {tabs.map((t, i) => <li><a role="tab" id={`tab-${t.id}`} href={`#${t.id}`} aria-controls={t.id} aria-selected={i === 0 ? 'true' : 'false'} tabindex={i === 0 ? 0 : -1}>{t.name}</a></li>)}
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <div class="panels">

      <section role="tabpanel" id="recipe" aria-labelledby="tab-recipe" tabindex="0">
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>The recipe</h2>
        <p>
          Every family starts from the same recipe, the elementary cellular automaton or ECA, written as six items:
        </p>
        <ol class="recipe" id="definition">
          <li><strong>cells</strong> <Tex tex="c_i" /> in a row, one for every integer <Tex tex="i" />;</li>
          <li><strong>states</strong> <Tex tex="S = \{0, 1\}" />;</li>
          <li><strong>the state</strong> of cell <Tex tex="c_i" /> at tick <Tex tex="t" />, written <Tex tex="s(c_i, t)" />;</li>
          <li><strong>a start</strong> <Tex tex="s(c_i, 0) = s_0(c_i)" />;</li>
          <li><strong>a neighbourhood</strong> <Tex tex="\mathcal{N}(c_i) = \{c_{i-1}, c_i, c_{i+1}\}" />;</li>
          <li><strong>a rule</strong> <Tex tex="\phi" /> that turns the three states of the neighbourhood into the next state:</li>
        </ol>
        <Tex block tex="s(c_i, t+1) = \phi\bigl(s(c_{i-1}, t),\, s(c_i, t),\, s(c_{i+1}, t)\bigr)." />
        <p>
          Three cells of two states make eight neighbourhoods, and a rule is a 0 or a 1 for each: <Tex tex="2^{2^3} = 256" /> rules,
          numbered 0 to 255 by reading the eight answers as a binary number <Tex tex="W(\phi)" />. A rule seen in a mirror,
          or with 0 and 1 swapped, draws the same pictures, which leaves 88 genuinely different ones. A few of those, like
          rule 110, are complex enough to compute anything.
        </p>
        <p>
          The five families each change one item. Three of them change item 6, the rule: the <a href="#asynchronous">asynchronous</a> family changes when a cell applies it, the <a href="#stochastic">stochastic</a> family
          lets a coin choose it, and the <a href="#rules">non-uniform</a> family lets it depend on the cell. The <a href="#states">multi-state</a> family changes item 2, the states, and the <a href="#neighbourhoods">extended-neighbourhood</a> family
          item 5, the neighbourhood. The paper's central
          diagram puts them round the elementary automaton, with an arrow from the centre for what each family changes
          and an arrow between neighbours for the identity that turns one into the other:
        </p>
      </div>
      <FamilyMap links={links} />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          There is a seventh item, hidden in the first. A computer holds a finite row, so the cells at the ends miss a
          neighbour, and something has to fill it in. The paper lists eight boundary conditions; a ring, where the last
          cell's right neighbour is the first cell, is the usual one and the only one in which no cell ever sees an edge.
          The choice can change the picture well beyond the edge cells:
        </p>
      </div>
      <Multiples kind="boundary" id="bc" rule={110} />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          One more pair of words runs through all the tabs. Some tools read a cellular automaton off its rule without
          running it: Langton's <Tex tex="\lambda" />, the share of neighbourhoods that do not map to 0, or the sensitivity,
          how often flipping one input flips the output. That is the <em>genotype</em>. Other tools read it off the pictures
          it draws: Wolfram's four classes (homogeneous, periodic, chaotic, complex), Lyapunov exponents that measure how a
          flipped cell spreads, entropies. That is the <em>phenotype</em>. Each family tab ends with which of these tools
          survive the bend, because that is where the price of realism shows.
        </p>
      </div>
      </section>

      <section role="tabpanel" id="asynchronous" aria-labelledby="tab-asynchronous" tabindex="0" hidden>
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>Asynchronous: no central clock</h2>
        <p>
          A cellular automaton claims to be fully local, and then it lets a global clock tell every cell when to move.
          Nature has no such clock. In an asynchronous cellular automaton (ACA) a cell at each tick either applies the rule
          or keeps its state. The most common version flips a coin for every cell: with the synchrony rate <Tex tex="\alpha \in [0, 1]" />,
        </p>
        <Tex block tex="s(c_i, t+1) = \begin{cases} \phi\bigl(\tilde{s}(\mathcal{N}(c_i), t)\bigr) & \text{with probability } \alpha, \\ s(c_i, t) & \text{with probability } 1 - \alpha, \end{cases}" />
        <p>
          where <Tex tex="\tilde{s}(\mathcal{N}(c_i), t)" /> is the tuple of states in the neighbourhood. At <Tex tex="\alpha = 1" /> this
          is the elementary automaton, at <Tex tex="\alpha = 0" /> nothing moves. Call <Tex tex="Z(t)" /> the set of cells that
          update at tick <Tex tex="t" />: here its size is binomial, about <Tex tex="\alpha N" />, and the family is the
          catalogue of ways to choose it. Fully asynchronous schemes update one cell at a time. A random independent ACA
          picks that cell at random, again and again. A random new sweep shuffles the row into a fresh order every <Tex tex="N" /> updates, so no cell goes twice before all have gone once; a random fixed sweep shuffles once and
          keeps the order; line by line updates the cells from left to right, so every cell sees one neighbour that has just
          moved. The same rule under all of them, one row per sweep, which is how such diagrams are usually compared:
        </p>
      </div>
      <Multiples kind="update" id="upd" rule={126} init="seed" />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Rule 126 on a clock draws nested triangles. Take the clock away and they fray, differently for every scheme:
          the random ones end in noise, while a fixed sweep and line by line settle into a checkerboard. Line by line is
          not even random, and it fills the row in its very first sweep: every cell reads its left neighbour's new state,
          so a change can run the whole length of the row at once.
        </p>
        <p>
          Fixed schemes carry a paradox. Updating cells in a fixed order, or with fixed periods, replaces the global clock
          with a global timetable: in the paper's words, one tyrant overthrows another. Such a deterministic <Tex tex="Z(t)" /> is
          mathematically convenient, though: Worsch showed that for all deterministic choices of <Tex tex="Z(t)" /> a single
          asynchronous automaton with a radius-1 rule can simulate all the others, so choosing the timetable for a rule is
          the same as choosing the rule for a timetable. And if <Tex tex="Z(t) = Z" /> never changes, the cells outside it
          simply never move: that is a <a href="#rules">non-uniform</a> automaton whose second rule is 204, the rule that
          changes nothing. The same trick, with a coin, is the <a href="#id-aca-sca">identity</a> with the stochastic family.
        </p>
        <p>
          Whether the steps are counted in computer time or physical time, with waiting times drawn from an exponential
          distribution, makes no real difference: time runs one way, so the two can always be mapped onto each other.
        </p>
        <h3>What survives</h3>
        <p>
          The genotype survives intact: <Tex tex="\lambda" /> and the other rule-table parameters describe <Tex tex="\phi" />,
          which is still one elementary rule. Global properties do not, since attractors and periodic orbits now depend on <Tex tex="Z(t)" /> too, and closed expressions exist only for special cases, such as the catalogue of rules whose
          periodic points do not depend on the order of a fixed sweep. The phenotype can be read in the usual way, as long as
          the diagrams are cropped to one update per cell on average, as above. It changes, though: asynchrony can move a
          rule to another Wolfram class, and even among the sixteen one-neighbour automata, simpler than the elementary
          ones, nine change their behaviour under some form of full asynchrony. Asynchrony is sometimes required, too: a
          whole row cannot be made to flash in step from every start by any synchronous deterministic rule, but it can
          by going beyond that, a problem <a href="/demos/thesis/#synchronisation">my thesis</a> takes up on networks.
        </p>
      </div>
      </section>

      <section role="tabpanel" id="stochastic" aria-labelledby="tab-stochastic" tabindex="0" hidden>
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>Stochastic: a coin picks the rule</h2>
        <p>
          Randomness drives the formation of structure in nature, morphogenesis for one, and a stochastic cellular
          automaton (SCA) lets every cell, every tick, flip a coin between two rules:
        </p>
        <Tex block tex="s(c_i, t+1) = \begin{cases} \phi\bigl(\tilde{s}(\mathcal{N}(c_i), t)\bigr) & \text{with probability } p, \\ \psi\bigl(\tilde{s}(\mathcal{N}(c_i), t)\bigr) & \text{with probability } 1 - p. \end{cases}" />
        <p>
          At <Tex tex="p = 1" /> this is the elementary automaton again. Make <Tex tex="\psi" /> rule 204, which returns the
          cell's own state, and the coin decides whether the cell moves or waits: that is the asynchronous automaton with <Tex tex="\alpha = p" />, the arrow <em>probabilistic update sequence</em> in the diagram. Below, both run from the
          same start with the same coins; change <Tex tex="\psi" /> and they part.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Identity kind="aca-sca" id="id-aca-sca" />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Two rules only disagree on some neighbourhoods, and on the others the coin makes no difference, so most SCAs are
          only partly random. At the other extreme, rule 0 against its complement 255 with <Tex tex="p = \tfrac12" /> ignores
          the neighbourhood altogether: pure noise. In between sits the majority SCA, rule 232 (follow the majority)
          against 51 (do the opposite of what you did), with its obvious link to how opinions spread.
        </p>
        <p>
          More generally, the chance of a 1 can depend on the neighbourhood itself. Eight neighbourhoods, eight
          probabilities:
        </p>
        <Tex block tex="s(c_i, t+1) = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{with probability } p\bigl(\tilde{s}(\mathcal{N}(c_i))\bigr), \\ 0 & \text{with probability } 1 - p\bigl(\tilde{s}(\mathcal{N}(c_i))\bigr), \end{cases} \qquad \mathbf{p} = (p_0, \dots, p_7) \in [0, 1]^8." />
        <p>
          The two-rule SCA is the special case where each probability is <Tex tex="p\,\phi + (1-p)\,\psi" /> of that
          neighbourhood, which is also how Fatès defines diploid automata. Other special cases keep only a few of the eight
          free, like the Domany–Kinzel automaton, whose equivalence to an Ising model matters to percolation theory.
        </p>
        <p>
          The <a href="#states">multi-state tab</a> adds a twist. Read the state of a cell as the probability that it is 1, and
          the stochastic automaton becomes a deterministic one on the continuous states <Tex tex="s_i \in [0, 1]" />:
        </p>
        <Tex block tex="\phi(s_{i-1}, s_i, s_{i+1}) = \sum_{n=0}^{7} p_n \prod_{j \in \{i-1,\, i,\, i+1\}} s_j^{\,b_j(n)}\, \bar{s}_j^{\,1 - b_j(n)}, \qquad \bar{s} = 1 - s," />
        <p>
          with <Tex tex="b_j(n)" /> the bit of neighbourhood <Tex tex="n" /> that belongs to cell <Tex tex="j" />. The
          figure runs the stochastic automaton once, runs it 200 times and averages, and runs the continuous automaton next
          to it. Pick a mix of two rules or set the eight chances by hand.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Mixture />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          The continuous automaton gets the first tick exactly right. After that it multiplies the chances of neighbouring
          cells as if they were independent, while two neighbours share most of their history. That makes it the mean-field
          approximation of the SCA rather than its exact twin; the paper presents the correspondence without this caveat, and
          the figure is my addition. From one cell, rule 22 half the time leaves few cells alive in most runs, and after 60
          ticks its continuous twin predicts about three times as many 1s as the runs actually have.
        </p>
        <p>
          The coin can also be global: all cells follow <Tex tex="\phi" /> together, except that with a small chance <Tex tex="\pi" /> the whole row takes a step of <Tex tex="\psi" /> instead, a noisy hiccup. For all 3825 pairs of
          genuinely different elementary rules, Roy and colleagues found that two chaotic rules can combine into a periodic
          one this way, and that the behaviour can jump at particular values of <Tex tex="\pi" />.
        </p>
        <h3>What survives</h3>
        <p>
          A rule-table parameter such as <Tex tex="\lambda" /> can be averaged over the two rules, weighted by the coin, but
          there is no reason the SCA should behave somewhere in between its two rules, and in general it does not. The
          global picture changes character: successive rows form a Markov chain, so the questions become ergodicity and
          absorption times rather than attractors. The phenotype loses two classes outright, since randomness disturbs any
          homogeneous or periodic pattern, and complexity can no longer mean computing power: there are no universal SCAs.
          What SCAs gain is phase transitions, sudden changes of behaviour as <Tex tex="p" /> passes a threshold, and a
          paradox: randomness can make a model more stable. A deterministic cellular automaton for the Ising model of a
          magnet runs into what Vichniac called a feedback catastrophe, nothing like a real magnet; a temperature-dependent
          probability per neighbourhood cures it.
        </p>
      </div>
      </section>

      <section role="tabpanel" id="states" aria-labelledby="tab-states" tabindex="0" hidden>
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>More states</h2>
        <p>
          Nature rarely comes in two states: a forest has trees, fire and ash, an epidemic has the susceptible, the
          infected and the recovered. A multi-state cellular automaton (MSCA) lets the state set <Tex tex="S" /> be any
          set, finite or not, with or without structure. The price is paid at once. With <Tex tex="k" /> states and <Tex tex="|\mathcal{N}|" /> neighbours there are <Tex tex="k^{k^{|\mathcal{N}|}}" /> rules: three states instead of two
          turn the 256 elementary rules into <Tex tex="3^{27} \approx 7.6 \times 10^{12}" />. A ternary rule is numbered like an
          elementary one, <Tex tex="\sum_{i=0}^{26} 3^i s_i" />, and most work sticks to totalistic rules, which only look at
          the sum of the neighbourhood: <Tex tex="k^{(k-1)|\mathcal{N}|+1}" /> of them, 2187 for three states.
        </p>
        <p>
          States can stand in for neighbours. Smith showed in 1971 that a cellular automaton with any neighbourhood is
          equivalent to one with a neighbourhood of three and more states, by pooling cells into blocks: a block and the two
          blocks beside it contain everything its own cells look at. So three neighbours are enough in general, and the
          trade is between the number of states, the size of the neighbourhood and the number of cells. Below is the paper's
          example: a two-state rule with five neighbours on 50 cells, and the 32-state rule with three neighbours on 10 cells
          that does the same thing. Try other block sizes, or any other of the <Tex tex="2^{32}" /> radius-2 rules.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Identity kind="pool" id="id-pool" />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          That is one half of the arrow <em>more time steps or fewer cells</em> between this family and the next. The other
          half trades time instead of cells: the paper shows the same five-neighbour rule simulated by an eight-state rule
          with three neighbours on the same number of cells, taking two ticks for each one.
        </p>
        <p>
          Let the number of states grow without bound and the states fill an interval: a continuous cellular automaton,
          close kin to coupled map lattices and to the finite-difference schemes that solve differential equations. Rucker
          objected that they are therefore redundant; what sets them apart is the habit of experiment rather than proof.
          The <a href="#stochastic">stochastic tab</a> shows one of them at work. Fuzzy cellular automata take a middle road:
          write an elementary rule in logic, rule 18 for example as
        </p>
        <Tex block tex="\phi(s_{i-1}, s_i, s_{i+1}) = (\bar{s}_{i-1} \wedge \bar{s}_i \wedge s_{i+1}) \vee (s_{i-1} \wedge \bar{s}_i \wedge \bar{s}_{i+1}),"/>
        <p>
          and let <em>and</em>, <em>or</em> and <em>not</em> act on degrees of truth between 0 and 1. The result can behave
          nothing like the original: fuzzy rule 90 from a random start settles on 1/2 everywhere, while Boolean rule 90 never
          settles at all.
        </p>
        <h3>What survives</h3>
        <p>
          Most of the genotype survives: Langton introduced <Tex tex="\lambda" /> for rules with at least five neighbours and
          four states in the first place, and roughly matched it to the four Wolfram classes. The sensitivity needs care,
          since flipping a state now has more than one outcome. Global properties are often out of reach, partly because
          the relevant questions are undecidable, unless the states have structure, such as linearity. The phenotype can
          still be sorted into classes, but local measures need the states to mean something: the density of 1s makes no
          sense when the states are S, I and R, and a change of state is no longer one thing. For continuous states, the
          tools of coupled map lattices serve better than those of cellular automata.
        </p>
      </div>
      </section>

      <section role="tabpanel" id="neighbourhoods" aria-labelledby="tab-neighbourhoods" tabindex="0" hidden>
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>Wider neighbourhoods</h2>
        <p>
          Hardly anything depends only and exactly on its two nearest neighbours, and hardly anything sits on a perfect line.
          An extended-neighbourhood cellular automaton (ENCA) keeps all neighbourhood information in an adjacency
          matrix <Tex tex="\mathbf{A}" />, with <Tex tex="A_{ij} \neq 0" /> when cell <Tex tex="c_j" /> is a neighbour
          of <Tex tex="c_i" />, and lets the rule read the neighbourhood at earlier ticks too:
        </p>
        <Tex block tex="\mathcal{N}(c_i) = \{ c_j \mid A_{ij} \neq 0 \}, \qquad s(c_i, t+1) = \phi\bigl(\tilde{s}(\mathcal{N}(c_i), 0), \dots, \tilde{s}(\mathcal{N}(c_i), t)\bigr)." />
        <p>
          The mildest version widens the neighbourhood to a radius <Tex tex="r" />, with <Tex tex="2r + 1" /> cells and <Tex tex="k^{k^{2r+1}}" /> rules; <Tex tex="r = 2" /> already gives over four billion. In two dimensions the usual
          neighbourhoods are von Neumann's, the four adjacent cells, and Moore's, which adds the diagonals, the simplest of
          Cole's templates
        </p>
        <Tex block tex="H^{(n)}_r = \Bigl\{ \mathbf{a} \Bigm| \sum_{i=0}^{n-1} |a_i| \le r \Bigr\}, \qquad J^{(n)}_r = \Bigl\{ \mathbf{a} \Bigm| \max_{0 \le i \le n-1} |a_i| \le r \Bigr\}," />
        <p>
          for the offsets <Tex tex="\mathbf{a}" /> of the neighbours in <Tex tex="n" /> dimensions. Any cellular automaton in <Tex tex="n" /> dimensions can be reduced to one in a single dimension with a wider neighbourhood, and one on a
          regular tiling can be simulated on another, hexagons for squares, at the cost of more states. Beyond tilings lie
          Voronoi cells, Penrose tilings and hyperbolic space, and finally network automata, where the cells are the nodes of
          any network, the neighbourhood of a node is whoever it is linked to and its size, the degree, differs from node to
          node. That is where <a href="/demos/cellular-automata/#life-on-graphs">the Game of Life on networks</a> of my
          Physica D paper lives.
        </p>
        <p>
          The arrow to the asynchronous family, <em>network wiring or ±1 dimension</em>, stands for several results.
          Golze built a synchronous automaton in one dimension more from an asynchronous one, by reading time as space;
          Aracena and colleagues showed equivalences of this kind for Boolean networks under different update schedules.
          The simplest instance I know is below, my own illustration rather than a construction from the paper. Updating
          the cells one by one from left to right is the same as updating them all at once, provided each cell reads the
          previous tick through the right wiring: cell <Tex tex="i" /> depends on every cell from the far end of the ring up
          to its right neighbour, through a composite rule of its own. Asynchrony becomes wiring, and a non-uniform one at
          that.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Identity kind="sweep" id="id-sweep" />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          The neighbourhood can also change over time, as in the structurally dynamic automata of Ilachinski, or reach into
          the past. An automaton with memory lets the rule read earlier ticks, and a memory of two ticks makes any rule
          reversible. Only six of the 256 elementary rules can be run backwards, all of them trivial, yet
        </p>
        <Tex block tex="s(c_i, t+1) = \begin{cases} \phi\bigl(\tilde{s}(\mathcal{N}(c_i), t)\bigr) & \text{if } s(c_i, t-1) = 0, \\ 1 - \phi\bigl(\tilde{s}(\mathcal{N}(c_i), t)\bigr) & \text{if } s(c_i, t-1) = 1 \end{cases}" />
        <p>
          can always be undone: given the two latest rows, the same formula gives back the row before them. Here the rule
          runs forward to the marks and then backward, and ends exactly where it began. Rule 30R is the paper's example;
          any rule works. Toffoli showed more: any cellular automaton can be simulated by a reversible one with one
          dimension more.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Multiples kind="reversible" id="rev" rule={30} init="seed" />
      <div class="measure">
        <h3>What survives</h3>
        <p>
          A wider neighbourhood leaves every definition intact and only makes the rule table longer: Langton's parameter was
          first studied with five neighbours. Global properties stay well defined but grow unwieldy, which is why totalistic
          rules are popular here. In a network the degree differs from node to node, so a rule table no longer fits, and the
          rule usually looks at the weighted share of neighbours in state 1,
        </p>
        <Tex block tex="s(c_i, t+1) = \phi\Bigl(\frac{1}{|\mathcal{N}(c_i)|} \sum_{c_j \in \mathcal{N}(c_i)} A_{ij}\, s(c_j, t)\Bigr)," />
        <p>
          which is open to analysis, only not the classical kind. Network science then does more for the genotype than
          cellular-automaton theory does. In the phenotype, Wolfram's classes carry over to two dimensions but not
          everything carries to three, network automata show all four classes, and anything with a direction, like a
          Lyapunov exponent, has to follow paths through the network instead. The wiring alone can cause a phase transition,
          and adding a memory of up to ten ticks changes the behaviour of almost half of the elementary rules.
        </p>
      </div>
      </section>

      <section role="tabpanel" id="rules" aria-labelledby="tab-rules" tabindex="0" hidden>
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>More rules</h2>
        <p>
          The last family drops the rule that all cells share. In a non-uniform cellular automaton (νCA) the rule depends on
          the cell and the tick; the simplest has two elementary rules and a set <Tex tex="Z(t)" /> of cells that follow the
          first:
        </p>
        <Tex block tex="s(c_i, t+1) = \begin{cases} \phi\bigl(\tilde{s}(\mathcal{N}(c_i), t)\bigr) & \text{if } i \in Z(t), \\ \psi\bigl(\tilde{s}(\mathcal{N}(c_i), t)\bigr) & \text{otherwise,} \end{cases} \qquad \nu(t) = \frac{|Z(t)|}{N}," />
        <p>
          with <Tex tex="\nu" /> the degree of non-uniformity, maximal at one half. With <Tex tex="\psi" /> rule 204 this is
          the <a href="#asynchronous">asynchronous</a> automaton with <Tex tex="\alpha = \nu" />, and the family is again a
          catalogue of ways to choose <Tex tex="Z(t)" />. Fixed once and for all, there are <Tex tex="2^N" /> allocations. A
          random one gives each cell a coin once; redraw it every tick and it is the <a href="#stochastic">stochastic</a> automaton,
          the arrow <em>probabilistic rule allocation</em>. A single rogue cell models a permanent fault; a periodic
          allocation, the first <Tex tex="\nu T" /> cells of every stretch of <Tex tex="T" />, is the one that admits
          formulas; a clustered one models regions, such as water and soil in a model of a forest fire.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Multiples kind="allocation" id="alloc" rule={153} psi={102} />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          The last panel lets the neighbourhood choose: rule 153 on the symmetric neighbourhoods 000, 010, 101 and 111, rule
          102 on the others. That is no longer non-uniform at all: it is the uniform rule 195, whose bits are the bits of 153
          where 153 applies and those of 102 elsewhere. The paper's example; any pair works.
        </p>
        <p>
          The allocation can also run by its own rule. Let a third elementary rule <Tex tex="\xi" /> update, every tick,
          which cells follow <Tex tex="\phi" />:
        </p>
        <Tex block tex="[\phi_i(t+1) = \phi] = \xi\bigl([\phi_{i-1}(t) = \phi],\, [\phi_i(t) = \phi],\, [\phi_{i+1}(t) = \phi]\bigr)." />
        <p>
          Now every cell carries two bits, its state and its rule, and both evolve by a uniform rule of the neighbourhood.
          Four combined states, three neighbours: a uniform quaternary automaton, the arrow <em>mapping onto product
          states</em>. The paper's figure uses <Tex tex="\phi = 110" />, <Tex tex="\psi = 170" /> and <Tex tex="\xi = 240" />,
          which shifts the allocation one cell to the right every tick; the rule number in the status line is the paper's,
          computed here from the three elementary rules, digit for digit.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Identity kind="nuca-msca" id="id-nuca-msca" />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          In general a νCA with <Tex tex="l" /> rules and <Tex tex="k" /> states is a uniform automaton with <Tex tex="kl" /> states
          and the same neighbourhood. The converse fails, because there are far fewer of the former:
        </p>
        <Tex block tex="k^{\,l k^{|\mathcal{N}|}}\, l^{\,l^{|\mathcal{N}|}} \ll (kl)^{(kl)^{|\mathcal{N}|}}," />
        <p>
          about <Tex tex="256^3 \approx 1.7 \times 10^7" /> against <Tex tex="4^{64} \approx 3.4 \times 10^{38}" /> here: most
          quaternary rules are not of this form, because the two bits of a combined state must each follow an elementary
          rule of their own. At the far end of the family, hybrid automata let a global parameter, such as the wind in a
          fire model or the temperature in Vichniac's Ising model, steer the rule: useful in applications, out of reach of
          formulas.
        </p>
        <h3>What survives</h3>
        <p>
          Much of the classical genotype rests on one rule table applied everywhere, and fails without it. Rule-table
          parameters can be averaged over the rules, but the paper suggests something less arbitrary: where a uniform
          automaton that simulates the νCA exists, as above, read the parameters off its rule table. The paper knew of no
          one who had tried this. Some global properties have been worked out, such as which νCAs built from elementary rules
          conserve the number of 1s or can be reversed. The phenotype is almost unexplored: two rules can already be
          allocated in about <Tex tex="2^N / N" /> ways on a ring of <Tex tex="N" /> cells, and they can cooperate in unpredictable ways.
          Nearly every local measure stays well defined, and one is new: sensitivity to the allocation itself. Give a single
          cell the other rule, like the rogue cell above, and ask how far that difference spreads. The simplest νCAs are also
          convolutional neural networks with their weights written down by hand, which <a href="/demos/thesis/#cnn">my thesis</a> uses to run many of them at once.
        </p>
      </div>
      </section>

      <section role="tabpanel" id="verdict" aria-labelledby="tab-verdict" tabindex="0" hidden>
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>What it adds up to</h2>
        <p>
          The five families build up to the paper's main conclusion:
        </p>
        <p class="claim">
          The variants of the cellular automaton are not a melting pot but a family tree: five families, each bending one
          item of the classical definition, tied together by exact mathematical identities, and in which every gain in
          realism is paid for in mathematical elegance.
        </p>
        <p>
          Three observations stand out. First, a paradox: the cellular automaton is meant to be strictly local, yet most
          of its families impose some global or random choice on the cells, a timetable, a coin, an allocation, a
          parameter. Second, the trade-off: more realism and more applications almost always come with less mathematical
          elegance and fewer general methods, as the table shows. Third, the overlap: the identities round the rim mean that
          one question can be attacked from several families at once, and they connect research communities that rarely
          read each other.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">family</th><th scope="col">bends</th><th scope="col">genotype: tools read off the rule</th><th scope="col">phenotype: tools read off the pictures</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row"><a href="#asynchronous">asynchronous</a></th><td>when a cell updates</td><td>rule-table parameters intact; global properties depend on the update order</td><td>readable after cropping to one update per cell; the Wolfram class can change</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><a href="#stochastic">stochastic</a></th><td>which rule a cell follows, by chance</td><td>averaged parameters need not predict anything; Markov chains replace attractors</td><td>no truly homogeneous or periodic behaviour, no universality; phase transitions in <Tex tex="p" /></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><a href="#states">multi-state</a></th><td>the states</td><td>most parameters survive; global properties often undecidable</td><td>classes still work; densities and changes of state need a meaning for the states</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><a href="#neighbourhoods">extended neighbourhood</a></th><td>who the neighbours are</td><td>definitions intact but tables grow; on networks, totalistic rules and network science</td><td>classes carry over, mostly; directions become paths; topology alone can cause phase transitions</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><a href="#rules">non-uniform</a></th><td>which rule a cell follows, by position</td><td>much of it lost; possibly recovered from a simulating uniform automaton</td><td>almost unexplored; local measures intact, plus sensitivity to the allocation</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          The paper ends on the two families it sees as best placed to reveal more: non-uniform automata and network
          automata, close to well-documented families, easy to simulate in bulk and widely applied. That is where the
          rest of my PhD went: the <a href="/demos/thesis/">thesis</a> studies Life-like rules on networks, how far
          their rule alone predicts their behaviour, and non-uniform automata as neural networks.
        </p>
        <p>
          A caveat about this page. The identities are exact and the figures check them cell for cell, but each family is
          represented by its simplest members, one-dimensional rows of two-state cells with two rules at most. The paper
          covers much more ground, from Penrose tilings to fuzzy logic, in words and references rather than simulations.
        </p>

        <h2>Read more</h2>
        <ul class="pubs">
          <li>
            <a href={doi}>A comprehensive taxonomy of cellular automata</a>
            <div class="meta"><strong>M. Rollier</strong>, K. M. C. Zielinski, A. J. Daly, O. M. Bruno, J. M. Baetens</div>
            <div class="meta">Communications in Nonlinear Science and Numerical Simulation 140, 108362, 2025 · <a href={arxiv}>preprint</a></div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s11047-018-9696-8">A survey of cellular automata: types, dynamics, non-uniformity and applications</a>
            <div class="meta">K. Bhattacharjee, N. Naskar, S. Roy, S. Das, Natural Computing 19, 2020. The closest earlier survey.</div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.physd.2021.133074">Progress, gaps and obstacles in the classification of cellular automata</a>
            <div class="meta">M. Vispoel, A. J. Daly, J. M. Baetens, Physica D 432, 2022. The genotype and phenotype tools this page keeps coming back to.</div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1406.0792">A guided tour of asynchronous cellular automata</a>
            <div class="meta">N. Fatès, 2014. The asynchronous family in depth.</div>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="meta">
          Keys once a figure has focus: n draws new coins or a new start. The address bar follows the settings, so a view
          can be linked.
        </p>
      </div>
      </section>

    </div>
